vendor/alpine/
--------------

An unmodified Alpine Linux 3.20.10 x86 minirootfs and five unmodified .apk
packages, redistributed under the terms of their respective licences.

    alpine-minirootfs-3.20.10-x86.tar.gz
    sha256 7a80c1cfc1a8ca542c0b553fc3d42b962ad72531ba28850aecc8473f3472dd7a

    packages/libncursesw-6.4_p20240420-r2.apk
    packages/ncurses-terminfo-base-6.4_p20240420-r2.apk
    packages/vim-9.1.0707-r0.apk
    packages/vim-common-9.1.0707-r0.apk
    packages/xxd-9.1.0707-r0.apk

Alpine Linux is a project of the Alpine Linux Development Team, distributed
from https://alpinelinux.org/. A minirootfs is an aggregation of separately
licensed components rather than a single work: musl is MIT, BusyBox is GPLv2,
Vim carries the Vim licence, and ncurses is MIT-like. The licence texts ship
inside the archive and the packages, which is where to read them.

Why these files are here rather than downloaded. The offline package-install
result the paper reports depends on installing from bytes the guest already
holds, with no network reachable from inside it. Fetching at run time would not
reproduce that, and pinning a URL would not survive the mirror rotating. The
checksum above is what makes the result reproducible byte for byte, so verify it
rather than trusting the file:

    sha256sum -c vendor/alpine/alpine-minirootfs-3.20.10-x86.tar.gz.sha256


vendor/busybox/
---------------

One unmodified BusyBox 1.35.0 binary for i686, statically linked against musl,
redistributed under GPLv2.

    busybox-1.35.0-i686
    sha256 27e409705b29937b9a6105038d9c2e3eb8dc3e2c8d606d22872dbde7f47c000f

Upstream publishes it at
https://busybox.net/downloads/binaries/1.35.0-i686-linux-musl/busybox, and the
corresponding source for this version is
https://busybox.net/downloads/busybox-1.35.0.tar.bz2. **GPLv2 section 3 requires
that source to be available to anyone who receives the binary**, which is what
that second link is for; keep it accurate if the binary is ever replaced.

Why it is here. The guest images this project boots ship a BusyBox built without
httpd, without nc, and without any other applet that can accept a connection, so
nothing in them can listen on a port. Serving a machine to another tab needs
something in the guest that can, and this is the smallest thing that works: one
static megabyte, no libc in the guest to satisfy, no package manager, and it
goes in over the 9p share like every other binary. Verify it rather than
trusting the file:

    sha256sum -c vendor/busybox/busybox-1.35.0-i686.sha256


vendor/v86/
-----------

The v86 emulator, version 0.5.445+gb0d8f2c, redistributed under BSD-2-Clause.
Only the two files a browser needs are here:

    libv86.js
    v86.wasm
    LICENSE     the copyright notice clause 2 requires to travel with them

Upstream is https://github.com/copy/v86. They are committed rather than
installed because a static host has no npm, and the page has to load the
emulator from the same origin that serves it.

src/device/v86.js is our own wrapper around v86's buffer interface. It is not
part of v86 and is covered by LICENSE at the root.


